export const MODEL_GUIDE = "## 'RCA Coverage' (lakehouse table dbo.rca_coverage)\nWhat the observer captured for one target over one document's window, and what it didn't, with why. The Incident RCA's evidence (ADR 0031): what the observer beside the workload captured, which NB_DatabaseHub_LiveActivity writes from its evidence documents (rca/history-tables.json).\nColumns:\n- 'RCA Coverage'[Run ID] string: The notebook's RUN_ID: session-<SESSION_ID> for a control session, as the other history tables.\n- 'RCA Coverage'[Document ID] string: The evidence document the row came from (documentId).\n- 'RCA Coverage'[Target Key] string: T1 to T5, or T4-replica.\n- 'RCA Coverage'[Window Start UTC] dateTime: Start of the window.\n- 'RCA Coverage'[Window End UTC] dateTime: End of the window.\n- 'RCA Coverage'[Captured] boolean: True when the sentinel ran for the whole window.\n- 'RCA Coverage'[Sources] string: Comma-separated capture IDs that returned rows.\n- 'RCA Coverage'[Gaps] string: One gap per line: what wasn't captured and why (a permission, a timeout, the export's budget).\n- 'RCA Coverage'[Report Date UTC] dateTime: The UTC date of the row's primary time; relates the table to Date, as the other history tables.\n\n## 'RCA Events' (lakehouse table dbo.rca_events)\nOne server-side event, or one count of identical events per minute: an error, an attention, a deadlock, a lock escalation, a tuning change, or the observer's own capture escalation. The Incident RCA's evidence (ADR 0031): what the observer beside the workload captured, which NB_DatabaseHub_LiveActivity writes from its evidence documents (rca/history-tables.json).\nColumns:\n- 'RCA Events'[Run ID] string: The notebook's RUN_ID: session-<SESSION_ID> for a control session, as the other history tables.\n- 'RCA Events'[Document ID] string: The evidence document the row came from (documentId).\n- 'RCA Events'[Event Time UTC] dateTime: When it happened; for a count, the start of its minute.\n- 'RCA Events'[Target Key] string: T1 to T5, or T4-replica.\n- 'RCA Events'[Kind] string: error, attention, deadlock, escalation, tuning, or capture.\n- 'RCA Events'[Event Name] string: The source event: the Extended Events event (error_reported, attention, xml_deadlock_report, database_xml_deadlock_report, lock_escalation), the counter (pg_stat_database.deadlocks), the tuning change, or for capture: escalated or relaxed.\n- 'RCA Events'[Session ID] int64: The session the event belongs to; for a deadlock, its victim.\n- 'RCA Events'[Program] string: As in RCA Sessions: the workload's own sessions only, without internal segments.\n- 'RCA Events'[Run Token] string: As in RCA Sessions.\n- 'RCA Events'[Login] string: The workload's own sessions only.\n- 'RCA Events'[Host] string: The workload's own sessions only.\n- 'RCA Events'[Module] string: The module the event's statement belongs to.\n- 'RCA Events'[Code] string: The SQL Server error number or the PostgreSQL SQLSTATE; for capture, the episode's ID.\n- 'RCA Events'[Severity] int64: The SQL Server error severity.\n- 'RCA Events'[Intercepted] boolean: True when the statement's own TRY...CATCH handled the error (error_reported's is_intercepted).\n- 'RCA Events'[Event Count] int64: 1 for one event; the count when identical events are counted per minute.\n- 'RCA Events'[Object] string: The object the event names: the escalated table, a deadlock's first resource, the tuned query.\n- 'RCA Events'[Lock Mode] string: The lock mode of an escalation.\n- 'RCA Events'[Detail] string: The event in plain words, normalized: the error message, the escalation's lock counts, the tuning state, or the signal and value that started or ended a capture episode.\n- 'RCA Events'[Deadlock JSON] string: For a deadlock: its graph reduced to JSON (rca/README.md, Deadlocks).\n- 'RCA Events'[Report Date UTC] dateTime: The UTC date of the row's primary time; relates the table to Date, as the other history tables.\nMeasures:\n- [Deadlocks (captured)] (RCA): Deadlocks the observer captured.\n- [Errors (captured)] (RCA): Errors the observer captured, intercepted ones included.\n- [Lock Escalations (captured)] (RCA): Lock escalations the observer captured.\n\n## 'RCA Queries' (lakehouse table dbo.rca_queries)\nNot written yet: the capture doesn't collect Query Store's statistics yet, so this table is empty, and its emptiness says nothing about a target's Query Store: never that a target has none (validate.ps1's T1.QUERYSTORE to T3.QUERYSTORE say whether it's on). Its grain, once a later release writes it: one Query Store plan over one phase of a capture episode. The Incident RCA's evidence (ADR 0031), which NB_DatabaseHub_LiveActivity writes from the observer's evidence documents (rca/history-tables.json).\nColumns:\n- 'RCA Queries'[Run ID] string: The notebook's RUN_ID: session-<SESSION_ID> for a control session, as the other history tables.\n- 'RCA Queries'[Document ID] string: The evidence document the row came from (documentId).\n- 'RCA Queries'[Target Key] string: T1, T2, or T3.\n- 'RCA Queries'[Episode ID] string: The observer's capture episode.\n- 'RCA Queries'[Phase] string: baseline or episode.\n- 'RCA Queries'[Interval Start UTC] dateTime: Start of the Query Store intervals summed.\n- 'RCA Queries'[Interval End UTC] dateTime: End of the Query Store intervals summed.\n- 'RCA Queries'[Query ID] int64: Query Store query_id.\n- 'RCA Queries'[Plan ID] int64: Query Store plan_id.\n- 'RCA Queries'[Query Hash] string: The query hash, as hex.\n- 'RCA Queries'[Module] string: The module the query belongs to.\n- 'RCA Queries'[Statement] string: The query text, normalized.\n- 'RCA Queries'[Executions] int64: Executions in the phase.\n- 'RCA Queries'[CPU (ms)] double: Total CPU time.\n- 'RCA Queries'[Duration (ms)] double: Total duration.\n- 'RCA Queries'[Logical Reads] double: Total logical reads.\n- 'RCA Queries'[Physical Reads] double: Total physical reads.\n- 'RCA Queries'[Log Bytes] double: Total log bytes used.\n- 'RCA Queries'[Max Used Memory (KB)] double: The largest memory grant used.\n- 'RCA Queries'[Max DOP] int64: The highest degree of parallelism.\n- 'RCA Queries'[Plans For Query] int64: How many plans the query had in Query Store.\n- 'RCA Queries'[Is Forced Plan] boolean: True when the plan is forced.\n- 'RCA Queries'[Plan Forcing Type] string: MANUAL or AUTO when forced.\n- 'RCA Queries'[Report Date UTC] dateTime: The UTC date of the row's primary time; relates the table to Date, as the other history tables.\n\n## 'RCA Replica' (lakehouse table dbo.rca_replica)\nOne replay sample from one side of T4's replication: the read replica's replay state and conflicts, or the primary's view of the standby. The Incident RCA's evidence (ADR 0031): what the observer beside the workload captured, which NB_DatabaseHub_LiveActivity writes from its evidence documents (rca/history-tables.json).\nColumns:\n- 'RCA Replica'[Run ID] string: The notebook's RUN_ID: session-<SESSION_ID> for a control session, as the other history tables.\n- 'RCA Replica'[Document ID] string: The evidence document the row came from (documentId).\n- 'RCA Replica'[At UTC] dateTime: When the sample was taken.\n- 'RCA Replica'[Target Key] string: T4-replica for the replica's side, T4 for the primary's.\n- 'RCA Replica'[Side] string: replica or primary.\n- 'RCA Replica'[Standby] string: The primary's name for the standby (pg_stat_replication.application_name).\n- 'RCA Replica'[Since Last Replayed (s)] double: now() minus pg_last_xact_replay_timestamp() on the replica.\n- 'RCA Replica'[Received Not Replayed (bytes)] double: pg_last_wal_receive_lsn() minus pg_last_wal_replay_lsn() on the replica.\n- 'RCA Replica'[Replay Lag (s)] double: pg_stat_replication.replay_lag on the primary.\n- 'RCA Replica'[Write Lag (s)] double: pg_stat_replication.write_lag on the primary.\n- 'RCA Replica'[Flush Lag (s)] double: pg_stat_replication.flush_lag on the primary.\n- 'RCA Replica'[Send Gap (bytes)] double: The primary's current WAL position minus what it sent.\n- 'RCA Replica'[WAL Bytes/s] double: WAL the primary generated per second since the previous sample (pg_stat_wal).\n- 'RCA Replica'[Startup Wait] string: The replica's startup (replay) process wait, wait_event_type:wait_event.\n- 'RCA Replica'[Conflict Relation] string: The table whose lock replay waits for, from pg_locks.\n- 'RCA Replica'[Conflict Wanted Mode] string: The lock mode replay waits for (AccessExclusiveLock).\n- 'RCA Replica'[Conflict Wait Start UTC] dateTime: When replay started to wait (pg_locks.waitstart).\n- 'RCA Replica'[Conflict Holder Session] int64: The replica query's pid that holds the conflicting lock.\n- 'RCA Replica'[Conflict Holder Mode] string: The holder's lock mode.\n- 'RCA Replica'[Conflict Holder Program] string: The holder's application name, as program_name in RCA Sessions.\n- 'RCA Replica'[Conflict Holder Run Token] string: As run_token in RCA Sessions.\n- 'RCA Replica'[Conflict Holder Statement] string: The holder's statement, normalized.\n- 'RCA Replica'[Conflicts Cancelled] int64: Queries the replica cancelled for conflicts since the previous sample (pg_stat_database_conflicts).\n- 'RCA Replica'[Max Standby Streaming Delay (s)] double: The replica's max_standby_streaming_delay.\n- 'RCA Replica'[Report Date UTC] dateTime: The UTC date of the row's primary time; relates the table to Date, as the other history tables.\nMeasures:\n- [Replica Replay Stall (s)] (RCA): The longest the read replica went without replaying a transaction, in seconds.\n\n## 'RCA Resource' (lakehouse table dbo.rca_resource)\nOne counter or resource sample, in long format: the sentinel's counters, and the resource samples of capture episodes. The Incident RCA's evidence (ADR 0031): what the observer beside the workload captured, which NB_DatabaseHub_LiveActivity writes from its evidence documents (rca/history-tables.json).\nColumns:\n- 'RCA Resource'[Run ID] string: The notebook's RUN_ID: session-<SESSION_ID> for a control session, as the other history tables.\n- 'RCA Resource'[Document ID] string: The evidence document the row came from (documentId).\n- 'RCA Resource'[At UTC] dateTime: When the sample was taken; for a delta, the end of its interval.\n- 'RCA Resource'[Target Key] string: T1 to T5, or T4-replica.\n- 'RCA Resource'[Source] string: The capture ID that read it (rca/capture/README.md).\n- 'RCA Resource'[Metric] string: The metric's name (rca/README.md, Resource metrics).\n- 'RCA Resource'[Value] double: The value: a gauge's reading, or a counter's delta over the interval.\n- 'RCA Resource'[Unit] string: count, percent, ms, bytes, or per second.\n- 'RCA Resource'[Report Date UTC] dateTime: The UTC date of the row's primary time; relates the table to Date, as the other history tables.\n\n## 'RCA Sessions' (lakehouse table dbo.rca_sessions)\nOne session in one sample of a blocking chain: every session that waits on another, and every session another waits on. The Incident RCA's evidence (ADR 0031): what the observer beside the workload captured, which NB_DatabaseHub_LiveActivity writes from its evidence documents (rca/history-tables.json).\nColumns:\n- 'RCA Sessions'[Run ID] string: The notebook's RUN_ID: session-<SESSION_ID> for a control session, as the other history tables.\n- 'RCA Sessions'[Document ID] string: The evidence document the row came from (documentId).\n- 'RCA Sessions'[Captured At UTC] dateTime: When the observer took the sample (UTC).\n- 'RCA Sessions'[Target Key] string: T1, T2, T3, T4, or T4-replica.\n- 'RCA Sessions'[Session ID] int64: The SQL Server session_id (SPID) or the PostgreSQL pid.\n- 'RCA Sessions'[Blocked By] string: Comma-separated IDs of the sessions this one waits on (SQL Server: blocking_session_id, including the documented negative owners; PostgreSQL: pg_blocking_pids, or the lock's holders from pg_locks). Null when it isn't blocked.\n- 'RCA Sessions'[Status] string: SQL Server: the request's status when the session has a request (running, runnable, suspended), else the session's (sleeping). PostgreSQL: pg_stat_activity.state (active, idle, idle in transaction).\n- 'RCA Sessions'[Wait Type] string: SQL Server wait_type, or PostgreSQL wait_event_type:wait_event.\n- 'RCA Sessions'[Wait (ms)] int64: How long the current wait has lasted.\n- 'RCA Sessions'[Wait Resource] string: SQL Server wait_resource; PostgreSQL's lock tag (locktype and its fields).\n- 'RCA Sessions'[Lock Resource Type] string: KEY, PAGE, RID, OBJECT, XACT (SQL Server); relation, tuple, transactionid (PostgreSQL).\n- 'RCA Sessions'[Lock Object] string: The locked table, [schema].[table] on SQL Server or schema.table on PostgreSQL, when the lock names one.\n- 'RCA Sessions'[Lock Index] string: The locked index, for KEY and PAGE locks.\n- 'RCA Sessions'[Lock Mode Wanted] string: The mode this session waits for.\n- 'RCA Sessions'[Lock Mode Held] string: The mode this session holds on the resource others wait for.\n- 'RCA Sessions'[Open Transactions] int64: Open transactions (SQL Server open_transaction_count; PostgreSQL 1 when xact_start is set).\n- 'RCA Sessions'[Transaction Age (ms)] int64: How long its oldest open transaction has been open.\n- 'RCA Sessions'[Idle (ms)] int64: How long an idle session (sleeping, or idle in transaction) has been idle.\n- 'RCA Sessions'[Login] string: The login or role. The workload's own sessions only; null for any other session.\n- 'RCA Sessions'[Host] string: The client host. The workload's own sessions only.\n- 'RCA Sessions'[Program] string: The application name as a DBA would see it, without the workload's internal segments (rca/README.md, Names). The workload's own sessions only.\n- 'RCA Sessions'[Run Token] string: An opaque token shared by the sessions of one workload run, so the rules can group them; it names nothing (rca/README.md, Names).\n- 'RCA Sessions'[Module] string: The stored procedure or function the statement belongs to: [schema].[name] on SQL Server, the called function on PostgreSQL.\n- 'RCA Sessions'[Statement] string: The running statement, or the last one for an idle session, normalized (literals replaced, at most 2,000 characters).\n- 'RCA Sessions'[Input Buffer] string: SQL Server's input buffer for an idle session (the last batch or RPC), normalized.\n- 'RCA Sessions'[Backend Type] string: PostgreSQL backend_type (client backend, startup, walreceiver, walsender).\n- 'RCA Sessions'[Is Workload Session] boolean: True for the demo workload's own sessions, the baseline's and any fault's alike, which keep text, login, and host; false for any other session, which arrives without them.\n- 'RCA Sessions'[Report Date UTC] dateTime: The UTC date of the row's primary time; relates the table to Date, as the other history tables.\nMeasures:\n- [Blocked Sessions (captured)] (RCA): The most sessions blocked in one sample in context.\n- [Head Blocker Idle (s)] (RCA): The longest a head blocker (a session others wait on that waits on no one) sat idle, in seconds.\n\n## 'RCA Waits' (lakehouse table dbo.rca_waits)\nNot written yet: the capture doesn't diff the wait statistics yet, so this table is empty, and its emptiness says nothing about the targets' waits or their Query Store; Telemetry Snapshots holds T1 to T3's waits by category. Its grain, once a later release writes it: one wait type's delta over one phase of a capture episode: the episode itself, or the baseline just before it. The Incident RCA's evidence (ADR 0031), which NB_DatabaseHub_LiveActivity writes from the observer's evidence documents (rca/history-tables.json).\nColumns:\n- 'RCA Waits'[Run ID] string: The notebook's RUN_ID: session-<SESSION_ID> for a control session, as the other history tables.\n- 'RCA Waits'[Document ID] string: The evidence document the row came from (documentId).\n- 'RCA Waits'[Target Key] string: T1, T2, or T3.\n- 'RCA Waits'[Episode ID] string: The observer's capture episode (rca_events kind capture, event_name escalated, code) the row belongs to.\n- 'RCA Waits'[Phase] string: baseline (the minutes before the episode) or episode.\n- 'RCA Waits'[Window Start UTC] dateTime: Start of the delta's window.\n- 'RCA Waits'[Window End UTC] dateTime: End of the delta's window.\n- 'RCA Waits'[Wait Type] string: The wait type.\n- 'RCA Waits'[Wait Category] string: Its category from Learn's wait categories mapping table.\n- 'RCA Waits'[Wait (ms)] int64: Wait time in the window.\n- 'RCA Waits'[Waiting Tasks] int64: Waits that ended in the window.\n- 'RCA Waits'[Signal Wait (ms)] int64: Signal wait time in the window.\n- 'RCA Waits'[Max Wait (ms)] int64: The longest single wait seen (the cumulative maximum when it rose in the window).\n- 'RCA Waits'[Report Date UTC] dateTime: The UTC date of the row's primary time; relates the table to Date, as the other history tables.\n\n## 'Telemetry Snapshots' (lakehouse table dbo.telemetry_snapshots)\nOne value per notebook run, analysis, target, resource, instance, database, metric, and bin, from dbo.telemetry_snapshots. Overlapping notebook runs store the same bin more than once; every measure keeps only the most recent capture of each bin (Snapshot Value).\nColumns:\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Run ID] string\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Source Notebook] string\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Captured At UTC] dateTime\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Analysis Key] string\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Target Key] string\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Resource Type] string: Azure resource type of the target: virtualMachines (T1), machines (T2), or servers/databases (T3).\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Resource Name] string: Azure resource name of the target.\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Instance Name] string: SQL Server instance name.\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Database Name] string: Database name, where the analysis is per database.\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Metric] string: The metric or wait category, for example AvgCPU, Processes blocked, Lock, or Buffer IO (PAGEIOLATCH).\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Bucket UTC] dateTime: Start of the telemetry bin (UTC). Empty for analyses with one row per window; the latest sample time for memory, growth, and coverage.\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Value] double\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Unit] string: Unit of Value, for example percent, rows, or MB.\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Window Start UTC] dateTime\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Window End UTC] dateTime\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Report Date UTC] dateTime\nMeasures:\n- [Snapshot Value] (Base): Base measure. Per target, analysis, metric, and bin, keeps only the most recent capture, then sums across instances and files inside that capture. Every telemetry measure builds on it.\n- [CPU (%)] (CPU): Average CPU per bin across the selected targets and period. On T3 the value is a percentage of the service tier's limit.\n- [CPU Peak (%)] (CPU): Highest per-bin CPU peak (MaxCPU) in the selection. On T3 the value is a percentage of the service tier's limit.\n- [Blocked Processes (peak)] (Locks and blocking): Highest number of blocked processes in any bin (gauge, maximum per bin).\n- [Active Transactions (peak)] (Locks and blocking): Highest number of open transactions in any bin (gauge, maximum per bin).\n- [Active Sessions (peak)] (Locks and blocking): Highest number of sessions in the ContosoCare database in any bin.\n- [Lock Wait (s)] (Locks and blocking): Seconds spent in the Lock wait category, summed over the selected bins (wait deltas are stored in milliseconds per bin).\n- [Deadlocks] (Locks and blocking): Deadlocks in the selected bins: per-bin deltas of the cumulative Number of Deadlocks/sec counter.\n- [Buffer IO Wait (s)] (Waits and IO): Seconds of Buffer IO page-latch waits (PAGEIOLATCH): tasks waiting for data pages to be read from storage.\n- [Log Rate Governor Wait (s)] (Waits and IO): Seconds of Log Rate Governor waits: Azure SQL Database's log-rate governance, which limits how fast a database writes its transaction log.\n- [Wait Time (s)] (Waits and IO): Seconds of non-idle wait time in the selection, for every wait category in context.\n- [Data and Log IO (MB)] (Waits and IO): Megabytes read and written on data and log files in the selected bins.\n- [Batch Requests] (Waits and IO): Batch requests in the selected bins: per-bin deltas of the cumulative Batch Requests/sec counter. The load axis of the explorer's scatter.\n- [Signal Value] (Explorer): The signal picked in the explorer's Signal slicer (CPU, average per bin, when none is picked), in the signal's own unit and format.\n- [Signal Threshold] (Explorer): The selected signal's attention threshold for the explorer's reference line; blank when the signal has none.\n- [Signal Peak] (Explorer): Highest per-bin value of the selected signal in context.\n- [Targets Reporting] (Estate): How many of the three SQL targets sent CPU telemetry in the selected period.\n- [Telemetry Lag at Capture (min)] (Estate): Age of the target's newest telemetry sample when the notebook captured the window (estate pulse), the larger across targets.\n- [Target Attention Signals] (Estate): The text twin of the health matrix colors, so color is never the only signal: every threshold the target crossed, or None.\n- [Notebook Runs Captured] (Freshness): Notebook runs that saved telemetry snapshots, over all history.\n- [Has Telemetry Bins] (Freshness): 1 when the lakehouse holds any per-bin telemetry, whatever the filters; drives every empty state.\n- [Coverage Tables (selected run)] (Coverage): Telemetry tables that each target sent, from the deployment notebook's coverage check in the validation run the readiness page selects.\n- [Coverage Last Sample Age (min)] (Coverage): Minutes between the target's newest telemetry sample and the end of the coverage window, in the selected validation run.\n- [Coverage Attention] (Coverage): Fresh, stale (older than 15 minutes, the deployment notebook's freshness rule), or not captured, with a glyph.\n- [Peak Norm RU (%)] (Cosmos DB): T5's peak normalized RU consumption in the selection (NormalizedRUConsumption, maximum per bin; analysis cosmosdb), 0 to 100 %: Database Hub's Peak Norm RU %. At 100 % T5 used all of its provisioned throughput in at least one second of a bin, and further requests are throttled (429).\n- [Cosmos DB Requests] (Cosmos DB): T5's requests in the selected bins (TotalRequests, count per bin), the throttled ones included: what the 429 rate divides by. Like every sum here, two captures of one period at different intervals both count.\n- [Throttled Requests (429s)] (Cosmos DB): T5's throttled requests in the selected bins: TotalRequests with status code 429 (request rate too large), Database Hub's Throttled Requests (429s). 0 when T5 served requests and none was throttled.\n- [429 Rate (%)] (Cosmos DB): Share of T5's requests in the selection that were throttled (429s), in percent: Database Hub's 429 Rate %.\n- [RU Consumed] (Cosmos DB): Request units T5 consumed in the selected bins (TotalRequestUnits, total per bin): Database Hub's Total RU consumed.\n- [Provisioned RU/s] (Cosmos DB): T5's provisioned throughput in RU/s in the selection (ProvisionedThroughput, maximum per hour): Database Hub's Provisioned RU/s, to compare with what it consumed.\n- [Cosmos DB Availability (%)] (Cosmos DB): T5's availability in the selection (ServiceAvailability, the average of its hourly values), as Database Hub's Performance shows it. A throttled request (429) isn't a failure, so throttling alone leaves it at 100 %.\n- [Cosmos DB Gateway Latency (ms)] (Cosmos DB): T5's server-side latency of gateway requests in the selection (ServerSideLatencyGateway, the average of its per-bin averages), in milliseconds. The workload runner uses gateway mode, so Database Hub's Avg Latency (direct mode) stays empty.\n- [Has Cosmos DB Bins] (Cosmos DB): 1 when the lakehouse holds any T5 (Azure Cosmos DB) metric, whatever the filters; drives the Cosmos DB view's empty state.\n\n## 'Validation Results' (lakehouse table dbo.validation_results)\nOne check per validation run and check ID, from dbo.validation_results: the Fabric-side checks of NB_DatabaseHub_Deployment, with ADR 0012 statuses. The Azure-side catalog stays in ./scripts/validate.ps1.\nColumns:\n- 'Validation Results'[Run ID] string: Validation run ID: the run's UTC start (yyyymmddThhmmssZ) and a random suffix, so sorting by it sorts by time.\n- 'Validation Results'[Source Notebook] string\n- 'Validation Results'[Check ID] string: The check's ID, for example FABRIC.P5.CAPACITY or HUB.TELEMETRY.T1.\n- 'Validation Results'[Area] string: The check's area, for example fabric, hub, or telemetry.\n- 'Validation Results'[Target Key] string\n- 'Validation Results'[Status Key] string\n- 'Validation Results'[Message] string: What the check found. Evaluation-only results keep their EVALUATION ONLY prefix (ADR 0012).\n- 'Validation Results'[Evidence] string: The check's evidence as redacted JSON.\n- 'Validation Results'[Remediation] string: The exact step that fixes the check, when it needs one.\n- 'Validation Results'[Documentation] string: Microsoft Learn or repository page for the check.\n- 'Validation Results'[Evaluation Only] boolean: True when the result comes from an evaluation-only path (ADR 0012).\n- 'Validation Results'[Checked At UTC] dateTime: When the check ran (UTC).\n- 'Validation Results'[Report Date UTC] dateTime\nMeasures:\n- [Selected Validation Run] (Readiness): The validation run the readiness page shows: the newest run in context (run IDs start with the run's UTC start, so the maximum is the latest).\n- [Is Selected Validation Run] (Readiness): Visual filter for the check queue: 1 when the row belongs to the selected validation run.\n- [Checks (selected run)] (Readiness): Checks in the selected validation run.\n- [Checks Failed (selected run)] (Readiness): Checks with status Fail in the selected validation run: 0 when there are none, so the strip tiles read 0; blank when no check of the run is in context.\n- [Checks Needing a Manual Step (selected run)] (Readiness): Checks with status Manual action required in the selected validation run: 0 when there are none, so the strip tiles read 0; blank when no check of the run is in context.\n- [Checks With Warnings (selected run)] (Readiness): Checks with status Warning in the selected validation run: 0 when there are none, so the strip tiles read 0; blank when no check of the run is in context.\n- [Checks Passed (selected run)] (Readiness): Checks with status Pass in the selected validation run: 0 when there are none, so the strip tiles read 0; blank when no check of the run is in context.\n- [Checks Not Supported (selected run)] (Readiness): Checks with status Not supported in the selected validation run: 0 when there are none, so the strip tiles read 0; blank when no check of the run is in context.\n- [Checks Not Selected (selected run)] (Readiness): Checks with status Not selected in the selected validation run: 0 when there are none, so the strip tiles read 0; blank when no check of the run is in context.\n- [Checks Needing Attention (selected run)] (Readiness): Checks that need attention (Fail, Manual action required, or Warning) in the selected validation run: 0 when there are none, so the strip tiles read 0; blank when no check of the run is in context.\n- [Checks Context] (Readiness): Reference line for the readiness strip: the run's check count.\n- [Readiness (%)] (Readiness): Pass share of the checks that apply and were selected (Not supported and Not selected excluded). Never turns green in the report.\n- [Worst Check Status Sort] (Readiness): Worst status of the checks in context: 1 Fail through 6 Not selected.\n- [Run Start UTC] (Readiness): First check time of the run in context (UTC); ranks the heat strip's latest six runs.\n\n## 'Analysis' (calculated)\nEvery analysis key that the notebooks write to telemetry_snapshots and analysis_findings, with its group and grain.\nColumns:\n- 'Analysis'[Analysis Key] string: Join key, the analysis value the notebooks write (for example cpu, wait_deltas, or telemetry_coverage).\n- 'Analysis'[Analysis] string: Business name of the analysis.\n- 'Analysis'[Analysis Group] string: The signal area: Estate, CPU, Locks and blocking, Throughput, Waits, IO, Memory, Storage, Readiness, Incidents, PostgreSQL, or Cosmos DB.\n- 'Analysis'[Grain] string: What one row means: per bin, per analysis window, latest sample, per incident, per agent beat, or per run.\n- 'Analysis'[Analysis Sort] int64\nRows:\n  { \"estate_pulse\", \"Estate pulse\", \"Estate\", \"Per analysis window\", 1 }\n  { \"cpu\", \"CPU\", \"CPU\", \"Per bin\", 2 }\n  { \"gauges\", \"Blocking and transaction gauges\", \"Locks and blocking\", \"Per bin\", 3 }\n  { \"sessions\", \"Active sessions\", \"Locks and blocking\", \"Per bin\", 4 }\n  { \"counter_deltas\", \"Throughput and deadlock counters\", \"Throughput\", \"Per bin\", 5 }\n  { \"wait_deltas\", \"Wait deltas\", \"Waits\", \"Per bin\", 6 }\n  { \"wait_profile\", \"Wait profile\", \"Waits\", \"Per analysis window\", 7 }\n  { \"storage_io\", \"Storage IO\", \"IO\", \"Per bin\", 8 }\n  { \"memory_clerks\", \"Memory clerks\", \"Memory\", \"Latest sample\", 9 }\n  { \"database_growth\", \"Database growth\", \"Storage\", \"Latest sample\", 10 }\n  { \"telemetry_coverage\", \"Telemetry coverage\", \"Readiness\", \"Latest sample per table\", 11 }\n  { \"telemetry_access\", \"Telemetry access\", \"Readiness\", \"Per run\", 12 }\n  { \"consumption\", \"Consumption\", \"CPU and waits\", \"Per analysis window\", 14 }\n  { \"real_time_dashboard\", \"Real-Time Dashboard\", \"Readiness\", \"Per run\", 16 }\n  { \"postgresql\", \"PostgreSQL (Azure Monitor)\", \"PostgreSQL\", \"Per bin\", 17 }\n  { \"cosmosdb\", \"Azure Cosmos DB (Azure Monitor)\", \"Cosmos DB\", \"Per bin\", 19 }\n  { \"rca_evidence\", \"Incident RCA evidence\", \"Readiness\", \"Per run\", 20 }\n\n## 'Date' (calculated)\nUTC calendar, 2026-01-01 to 2030-12-31, marked as the date table. Every fact table relates to it through Report Date UTC, the UTC date of the row's primary time, so a midnight-spanning incident falls on its start date.\nColumns:\n- 'Date'[Date] dateTime: The UTC calendar date.\n- 'Date'[Year] int64: Calendar year.\n- 'Date'[Month Key] int64\n- 'Date'[Month] string: Month and year, for example Oct 2026, sorted by calendar order.\n- 'Date'[Day] int64: Day of the month.\n- 'Date'[Date Label] string: The date as a label, for example 21 Oct 2026.\n\n## 'Signal' (calculated)\nThe signals that the Signal explorer can show. Disconnected on purpose: it only selects which measure Signal Value returns, with its unit, format, and attention threshold.\nColumns:\n- 'Signal'[Signal Key] string: The key that Signal Value switches on.\n- 'Signal'[Signal] string: The signal's name as the explorer lists it.\n- 'Signal'[Unit] string: Unit of the signal's values: %, processes, s, deadlocks, transactions, MB, requests, or sessions.\n- 'Signal'[Signal Family] string: The incident signal family the signal belongs to: cpu, blocking, io, log_rate, or activity.\n- 'Signal'[Format String] string: Format string for Signal Value's dynamic format.\n- 'Signal'[Attention Threshold] double: Value at which a bin needs attention; 0 means no threshold line.\n- 'Signal'[Signal Sort] int64\nRows:\n  { \"cpu\", \"CPU, average per bin\", \"%\", \"cpu\", \"#,0.0\"\" %\"\"\", 80, 1 }\n  { \"cpu_peak\", \"CPU, peak per bin\", \"%\", \"cpu\", \"#,0.0\"\" %\"\"\", 80, 2 }\n  { \"blocked\", \"Blocked processes, peak per bin\", \"processes\", \"blocking\", \"#,0\", 1, 3 }\n  { \"lock_wait\", \"Lock waits per bin\", \"s\", \"blocking\", \"#,0.0\", 1, 4 }\n  { \"deadlocks\", \"Deadlocks per bin\", \"deadlocks\", \"blocking\", \"#,0\", 1, 5 }\n  { \"transactions\", \"Active transactions, peak per bin\", \"transactions\", \"blocking\", \"#,0\", 0, 6 }\n  { \"buffer_io_wait\", \"Buffer IO (PAGEIOLATCH) waits per bin\", \"s\", \"io\", \"#,0.0\", 1, 7 }\n  { \"io_mb\", \"Data and log IO per bin\", \"MB\", \"io\", \"#,0.0\", 0, 8 }\n  { \"log_rate_wait\", \"Log Rate Governor waits per bin\", \"s\", \"log_rate\", \"#,0.0\", 0.1, 9 }\n  { \"batch_requests\", \"Batch requests per bin\", \"requests\", \"activity\", \"#,0\", 0, 10 }\n  { \"sessions\", \"Active sessions in ContosoCare, peak per bin\", \"sessions\", \"activity\", \"#,0\", 0, 11 }\n\n## 'Status' (calculated)\nThe six ADR 0012 statuses, worst first: Fail, Manual action required, Warning, Pass, Not supported, and Not selected. Only the first three need attention and carry a color; the rest stay graphite.\nColumns:\n- 'Status'[Status] string: The status word, sorted by severity.\n- 'Status'[Status Short] string: Short status word for chips and tiles.\n- 'Status'[Status Sort] int64\n- 'Status'[Needs Attention] string: Yes for Fail, Manual action required, and Warning; No for the other three.\n- 'Status'[Needs Attention Flag] int64\n- 'Status'[Status Glyph] string: The status glyph that pairs with its color, so color is never the only signal: ✕ ⚑ ▲ ✓ ⊘ ○.\n- 'Status'[Status Color] string: Hex color for conditional formatting: red, cyan, or amber for attention statuses, graphite otherwise.\n- 'Status'[Status Meaning] string: What the status means (ADR 0012).\nRows:\n  { \"Fail\", \"Fail\", 1, \"Yes\", 1, \"✕\", \"#FF4D5A\", \"A selected capability is broken, or a prerequisite is missing\" }\n  { \"Manual action required\", \"Manual\", 2, \"Yes\", 1, \"⚑\", \"#8CEEEE\", \"A documented manual step is pending\" }\n  { \"Warning\", \"Warning\", 3, \"Yes\", 1, \"▲\", \"#FFB020\", \"It works, but with a risk to address\" }\n  { \"Pass\", \"Pass\", 4, \"No\", 0, \"✓\", \"#8C8C9C\", \"Functionally verified\" }\n  { \"Not supported\", \"Not supported\", 5, \"No\", 0, \"⊘\", \"#8C8C9C\", \"The docs say the capability doesn't apply\" }\n  { \"Not selected\", \"Not selected\", 6, \"No\", 0, \"○\", \"#8C8C9C\", \"The operator turned the capability off\" }\n\n## 'Target' (calculated)\nThe demo's targets and platform scopes. T1, T2, and T3 are the SQL targets; T4 and T4-replica are the PostgreSQL flexible server and its read replica, whose Azure Monitor metrics the live-activity notebook saves when the scripts pass them (ADR 0025); T5 is the Azure Cosmos DB account, whose Azure Monitor metrics it saves the same way (ADR 0027); the other rows are the scopes that validation checks report on. Sorted in a fixed T1-to-T5 order; never colored by hue in the report.\nColumns:\n- 'Target'[Target Key] string: Join key, the target value the notebooks write (T1, T2, T3, T4, T4-replica, T5, all, fabric, subscription, tenant, github, repo).\n- 'Target'[Target] string: Display name with the platform, for example \"T1 · SQL Server on Azure VM\".\n- 'Target'[Target Short] string: Short label for tiles, slicers, and table rows: T1, T2, T3, or the scope's name.\n- 'Target'[Target Kind] string: SQL target, PostgreSQL target, Cosmos DB target, All targets, or Platform scope.\n- 'Target'[Platform] string: What the target runs on.\n- 'Target'[Target Sort] int64\nRows:\n  { \"T1\", \"T1 · SQL Server on Azure VM\", \"T1\", \"SQL target\", \"SQL Server 2022 on an Azure VM\", 1 }\n  { \"T2\", \"T2 · Arc-enabled SQL Server\", \"T2\", \"SQL target\", \"Arc-enabled SQL Server in a nested Hyper-V guest\", 2 }\n  { \"T3\", \"T3 · Azure SQL Database\", \"T3\", \"SQL target\", \"Azure SQL Database\", 3 }\n  { \"T4\", \"T4 · Azure Database for PostgreSQL\", \"T4\", \"PostgreSQL target\", \"Azure Database for PostgreSQL flexible server\", 4 }\n  { \"T4-replica\", \"T4 · read replica\", \"T4 replica\", \"PostgreSQL target\", \"Same-region read replica of T4, during a session\", 5 }\n  { \"T5\", \"T5 · Azure Cosmos DB\", \"T5\", \"Cosmos DB target\", \"Azure Cosmos DB for NoSQL account\", 6 }\n  { \"all\", \"All targets\", \"All\", \"All targets\", \"Every SQL target\", 7 }\n  { \"fabric\", \"Fabric workspace\", \"Fabric\", \"Platform scope\", \"Fabric workspace and lakehouse\", 8 }\n  { \"subscription\", \"Azure subscription\", \"Subscription\", \"Platform scope\", \"Azure subscription and resource group\", 9 }\n  { \"tenant\", \"Fabric tenant\", \"Tenant\", \"Platform scope\", \"Tenant settings\", 10 }\n  { \"github\", \"GitHub\", \"GitHub\", \"Platform scope\", \"GitHub repository and Actions\", 11 }\n  { \"repo\", \"Repository\", \"Repo\", \"Platform scope\", \"Static checks\", 12 }\n\n## Relationships (many-to-one: filters flow from the dimension to the fact)\n- 'Validation Results'[Target Key] -> 'Target'[Target Key]\n- 'Validation Results'[Status Key] -> 'Status'[Status]\n- 'Validation Results'[Report Date UTC] -> 'Date'[Date]\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Target Key] -> 'Target'[Target Key]\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Analysis Key] -> 'Analysis'[Analysis Key]\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Report Date UTC] -> 'Date'[Date]\n- 'RCA Sessions'[Target Key] -> 'Target'[Target Key]\n- 'RCA Sessions'[Report Date UTC] -> 'Date'[Date]\n- 'RCA Waits'[Target Key] -> 'Target'[Target Key]\n- 'RCA Waits'[Report Date UTC] -> 'Date'[Date]\n- 'RCA Events'[Target Key] -> 'Target'[Target Key]\n- 'RCA Events'[Report Date UTC] -> 'Date'[Date]\n- 'RCA Queries'[Target Key] -> 'Target'[Target Key]\n- 'RCA Queries'[Report Date UTC] -> 'Date'[Date]\n- 'RCA Resource'[Target Key] -> 'Target'[Target Key]\n- 'RCA Resource'[Report Date UTC] -> 'Date'[Date]\n- 'RCA Replica'[Target Key] -> 'Target'[Target Key]\n- 'RCA Replica'[Report Date UTC] -> 'Date'[Date]\n- 'RCA Coverage'[Target Key] -> 'Target'[Target Key]\n- 'RCA Coverage'[Report Date UTC] -> 'Date'[Date]";

/** The measures that read the demo's answer key, directly or through another measure: run_dax refuses them (ADR 0031). */
export const ANSWER_KEY_MEASURES: readonly string[] = ["Blocking Incidents","CPU In Incident Window","Estate Headline","Finding In Focus Window","Findings","Findings Needing Attention","Focus Baseline","Focus Correlation Status","Focus Duration (min)","Focus Evidence Sentence","Focus Incident Key","Focus Peak","Focus Peak Delta Label","Focus Peak Label","Focus Peak Point","Focus Range End UTC","Focus Range Peak","Focus Range Start UTC","Focus Resource Name","Focus Scenario","Focus Signal Family","Focus Signal Label","Focus Signal Raw","Focus Signal Value","Focus Source","Focus Target Key","Focus Threshold","Focus Verdict","Focus Verdict Rail Color","Focus Window Band","Focus Window End UTC","Focus Window Start UTC","Has Incident","History Freshness Label","History Freshness Rail Color","History Freshness Status","Hours Since Latest Capture","In Incident Window","Incident Headline","Incident Narrative","Incident Timeline Alt Text","Incidents","Injected Faults","Is Latest Finding Capture","Latest Capture UTC","Scenario Requests","Signal Value In Incident Window","Target Rail Color","Target Tile Context","Throttling Episodes","Worst Finding Status Sort"];

/** Query Store's evidence (the sidecar's tables), compact, from rca/history-tables.json: the chat gets it only when the model has them. */
export const SIDECAR_GUIDE = "## Query Store's evidence (the sidecar's tables)\nWhat the capture read of Query Store on T1 to T4, by Query Store's 15-minute intervals, and of T4's client connections in a connection episode: six tables, related to 'Target' and 'Date' as the others are. The capture reads an interval more than once, and two sessions can each read it, so a table holds several reads of one interval: use these measures, which take each interval's latest read, and never sum the tables' numeric columns. explain_queries reads them with its rules (the top queries, plan changes, forcings, and waits by category, against the hour before): call it first, and run_dax for what it doesn't answer. T4's query IDs are 64-bit, past what a number holds exactly: return them as text, CONVERT ( 'RCA Query Stats'[Query ID], STRING ).\nMeasures:\n- [Query CPU (ms)] on 'RCA Query Stats': Query Store's CPU time in context, in milliseconds: the latest read of each target, interval, login, query, and plan, summed, so several reads of one interval, or two sessions' reads of it, count it once.\n- [Query Duration (ms)] on 'RCA Query Stats': Query Store's elapsed time in context, in milliseconds: the latest read of each target, interval, login, query, and plan, summed.\n- [Query Executions] on 'RCA Query Stats': Query Store's executions in context: the latest read of each target, interval, login, query, and plan, summed.\n- [Query Wait (ms)] on 'RCA Query Stats': Query Store's wait time in context, every category, in milliseconds (PostgreSQL's an estimate from its samples): the latest read of each target, interval, login, query, and plan, summed.\n- [Forced Plans (captured)] on 'RCA Query Plans': Plans forced in context as their latest read saw them, by sys.sp_query_store_force_plan or automatic plan correction.\n- [Plan Forcings (captured)] on 'RCA Query Events': Plans the capture saw forced in context, manually or by automatic plan correction.\n- [Connections Held (captured)] on 'RCA Connections': The most client connections one sample of a connection episode found on T4 in context, every program and state together.\nAn interval counts for some minutes when it overlaps them. T3's top queries by CPU in them:\nEVALUATE TOPN ( 10, SUMMARIZECOLUMNS ( 'RCA Query Stats'[Query ID], 'RCA Query Stats'[Module], TREATAS ( { \"T3\" }, 'Target'[Target Key] ), FILTER ( ALL ( 'RCA Query Stats'[Interval Start UTC], 'RCA Query Stats'[Interval End UTC] ), 'RCA Query Stats'[Interval Start UTC] < <to> && 'RCA Query Stats'[Interval End UTC] > <from> ), \"CpuMs\", [Query CPU (ms)], \"Executions\", [Query Executions] ), [CpuMs], DESC )\nColumns to group or filter by:\n- 'RCA Query Stats', one plan's statistics over one interval: [Interval Start UTC] (filter minutes on it), [Interval End UTC], [Query ID], [Plan ID] (0 on T4 when it keeps no plans), [Login] (PostgreSQL's role; blank on SQL Server), [Module] ([schema].[name]; blank for an ad hoc statement), [Statement] (normalized).\n- 'RCA Query Waits', one plan's wait category over one interval, its three largest; no measure: explain_queries totals them: [Interval Start UTC], [Query ID], [Plan ID], [Wait Category] (SQL Server's: Lock, CPU, Buffer IO, Parallelism, ...), [Wait Event] (PostgreSQL's: transactionid, relation, ...).\n- 'RCA Query Plans', one plan as one read saw it, T1 to T3: [Query ID], [Plan ID], [Compiled UTC], [Is Forced Plan], [Forcing Type] (MANUAL, AUTO, or NONE), [Is Parallel].\n- 'RCA Query Events', one change the capture saw: [Event Time UTC], [Kind] (forced, unforced, state, or tuning), [State] (after the change), [Query ID], [Plan ID], [Forcing Type].\n- 'RCA Query Coverage', what the capture read of Query Store, and what it couldn't: [Window Start UTC], [Window End UTC], [Query Store State], [Read From UTC], [Read To UTC], [Pending From UTC] (T4's minutes not yet published when read), [Pending To UTC], [Gaps] (one per line, each with why).\n- 'RCA Connections', one group of T4's connections in one sample: [Captured At UTC] (the sample), [Program], [Login], [State] (active, idle, idle in transaction, ...).";
